---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ProjectInvitation from '../components/ProjectInvitation.astro';
import { getPublishedPieces } from '../lib/lab';
import { HARNESS_SLUG, GUIDE_PATH } from '../lib/workflow-brief';
import '../styles/visitor.css';
const hasWorkflowBrief = (await getPublishedPieces()).some(piece => piece.data.slug === HARNESS_SLUG);
const steps = [
  {title:'Understand the situation',text:'Describe the workflow, its users, the current baseline and the constraints. Clarify the outcome that would make the work useful.',output:'A shared problem statement and the conditions that matter.'},
  {title:'Define one testable question',text:'Agree the scope, accountable owner, available evidence and acceptance conditions. Identify what remains outside the work.',output:'A bounded brief with a clear basis for review.'},
  {title:'Build and evaluate',text:'Use an experiment, prototype or implementation appropriate to the question. Compare against a baseline and examine representative failure cases.',output:'An inspectable artifact and results with their conditions.'},
  {title:'Decide what follows',text:'Review what was established and what remains uncertain. Continue, change direction, narrow the scope or stop when the evidence calls for it.',output:'A reasoned next-stage decision.'},
  {title:'Make the work usable',text:'For the agreed use, define documentation, validation, handover and ownership. Integration, monitoring and maintenance must have responsible people.',output:'The material and responsibilities needed to carry the work forward.'},
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Our approach | Start with one useful question | Fidret" description="How a bounded project begins: understand the workflow, agree the evidence, build and evaluate, then decide the next stage with clear responsibilities." canonical="https://fidret.com/approach/">
  <main tabindex="-1" id="main">
    <header class="visitor-hero shell"><h1>Start with one useful question.</h1><p class="visitor-lead">Bring the workflow, the outcome you want to improve and the constraints that matter.</p><p>The first task is to understand what would make a useful result, what can credibly be tested and where responsibility belongs.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Discuss a project</a></header>
    <section class="collaboration-section shell" aria-labelledby="collaboration-title"><div class="visitor-section-heading"><h2 id="collaboration-title">A clearly scoped piece of work</h2><p>This is our working approach. Scope and deliverables depend on the actual question, access and evidence.</p></div><ol class="collaboration-steps">{steps.map(step=><li><h3>{step.title}</h3><p>{step.text}</p><p class="step-output"><strong>Useful output:</strong> {step.output}</p></li>)}</ol></section>
    <section class="work-evidence inverse"><div class="visitor-split shell"><h2>Match the claim to the evidence.</h2><div><p>An architecture, a software experiment, a supervised pilot and a deployed system establish different things. The conditions and unanswered questions belong beside the result.</p><p>For physical systems, operating limits, qualified oversight and recovery require their own validation. A software check alone does not establish that equipment is ready to act.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/">Inspect how we present the evidence</a></div></div></section>
    <section class="visitor-split shell"><h2>Make the reasoning usable.</h2><div><p>The signal experiment pairs a small rule with its baseline, replay and limitations. It is one example of making a result open to inspection.</p><a class="text-link" href="/explorations/signal-freshness/method/">Read the experiment method</a>{hasWorkflowBrief && <><p>For an agent workflow, the editable brief helps define purpose, context, authority, evidence and recovery before widening access.</p><a class="text-link" href={GUIDE_PATH}>Plan one agent workflow</a></>}<p><a class="text-link" href="/charter/">Read our company commitments</a></p></div></section>
    <ProjectInvitation title="Start with your situation." />
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
